      "derivations",
      2,
      "claim"
    ],
    "before": "By [F5], a product-Borel set $E$ is measurable for $\\mathcal B([0,T])\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$. Its sections $E_t$ are Borel by [F6]. Since $t\\mapsto\\mu_t$ is a probability kernel by step 1.2, [F7] applied to $\\mathbf1_E$ shows that $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(E_t)$ is measurable. Thus $M(E)=\\int_0^T\\mu_t(E_t)dt$ is defined; $M(\\varnothing)=0$ and $M([0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d)=\\int_0^T1dt=T$.",
    "after": "By [F5], a product-Borel set $E$ is measurable for $\\mathcal B([0,T])\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$. Its sections $E_t$ are Borel by [F6]. Since $t\\mapsto\\mu_t$ is a probability kernel by step 2.1, [F7] applied to $\\mathbf1_E$ shows that $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(E_t)$ is measurable. Thus $M(E)=\\int_0^T\\mu_t(E_t)dt$ is defined; $M(\\varnothing)=0$ and $M([0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d)=\\int_0^T1dt=T$.",
    "paragraph_identity_map": "lem-spacetime-law-of-a-narrow-probability-curve"
  },
