    "target": "research/machine-learning-all-101-2026-10-19-batch-50.proof-contracts.json",
    "path": [
      "contracts",
      "lem-spacetime-law-of-a-narrow-probability-curve",
      "derivations",
      3,
      "claim"
    ],
    "before": "If $E_j$ are disjoint product-Borel sets, their sections are disjoint and $\\mu_t((\\bigcup_jE_j)_t)=\\sum_j\\mu_t((E_j)_t)$. Integrating the increasing partial sums and using [F8] gives $M(\\bigcup_jE_j)=\\sum_jM(E_j)$; with step 1.2 this makes $M$ a finite Borel measure. For an indicator, the iterated-integral formula is the definition of $M$; finite nonnegative simple combinations give it for simple functions. For arbitrary nonnegative product-Borel $f$, use [F9] to choose $s_n\\uparrow f$ and apply [F8] to both $M$ and the time-section integrals. This proves $\\int f\\,dM=\\int_0^T\\int f(t,x)d\\mu_t(x)dt$.",
    "after": "If $E_j$ are disjoint product-Borel sets, their sections are disjoint and $\\mu_t((\\bigcup_jE_j)_t)=\\sum_j\\mu_t((E_j)_t)$. Integrating the increasing partial sums and using [F8] gives $M(\\bigcup_jE_j)=\\sum_jM(E_j)$; with step 2.1 this makes $M$ a finite Borel measure. For an indicator, the iterated-integral formula is the definition of $M$; finite nonnegative simple combinations give it for simple functions. For arbitrary nonnegative product-Borel $f$, use [F9] to choose $s_n\\uparrow f$ and apply [F8] to both $M$ and the time-section integrals. This proves $\\int f\\,dM=\\int_0^T\\int f(t,x)d\\mu_t(x)dt$.",
    "paragraph_identity_map": "lem-spacetime-law-of-a-narrow-probability-curve"
  },
