    "before": "Every event in $\\sigma(X_0)$ has the form $\\{X_0\\in B\\}$ for some $B\\subseteq\\mathcal X$, since these preimages form a sigma-algebra and generate $\\sigma(X_0)$. For every such event, step 4.2 gives $$\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}f(X_k)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta=\\sum_{x\\in B}q_0(x)\\sum_yK_\\theta^k(x,y)f(y)=\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}g_f(X_0)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta,$$ where terms with $q_0(x)=0$ vanish. The integrability and measurability from step 4.2 and this event-integral identity satisfy [F12], so $g_f(X_0)$ is the asserted conditional-expectation version.",
    "after": "Every event in $\\sigma(X_0)$ has the form $\\{X_0\\in B\\}$ for some $B\\subseteq\\mathcal X$, since these preimages form a sigma-algebra and generate $\\sigma(X_0)$. For every such event, step 2.2 gives $$\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}f(X_k)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta=\\sum_{x\\in B}q_0(x)\\sum_yK_\\theta^k(x,y)f(y)=\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}g_f(X_0)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta,$$ where terms with $q_0(x)=0$ vanish. The integrability and measurability from step 2.2 and this event-integral identity satisfy [F12], so $g_f(X_0)$ is the asserted conditional-expectation version.",
